<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Badr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/624">624</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Day of the Criterion, Yawm al-Furqan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Badr was fought on 13 March 624 (17 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a>, 2 AH) near Badr, in present-day Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Muhammad, with an army of about 313 men, defeated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quraysh">Quraysh</a> force of around 1300 led by Amr ibn Hisham, known to Muslims as Abu Jahl. It began when Muhammad set out to intercept a Meccan caravan under Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, which escaped by another route. About 70 Meccans were killed, including Amr ibn Hisham and Umayya ibn Khalaf, and about 70 were taken prisoner. Muslim tradition calls it a victory owed to divine intervention; other sources credit Muhammad's strategy.</p></div>
